# Project goal

Build a server-rendered UK government service using Python and Django that produces HTML compatible with GOV.UK Frontend.

The project demonstrates that GOV.UK Frontend does not depend on Nunjucks, Node.js, or a client-side frontend framework.

Official GOV.UK Frontend fixtures belonging to the installed version define rendering correctness.

The target is 100% fixture parity for every implemented component.

## Architecture

```text
GOV.UK Frontend fixture
        |
        v
Python component options
        |
        v
Django template tag / inclusion tag
        |
        v
Django Template
        |
        v
Server-rendered HTML
        |
        v
DOM comparison
        |
        v
Expected GOV.UK HTML
```

## Technology

Use:

- current supported Python
- Django
- Django Templates
- pytest
- pytest-django
- BeautifulSoup
- html5lib
- PyYAML
- mypy
- Ruff

Study existing projects such as `govuk-frontend-django` for established ecosystem approaches.

Do not blindly copy another implementation.

The official GOV.UK Frontend fixtures remain authoritative.

## GOV.UK Frontend

Pin an explicit GOV.UK Frontend version.

Use official GOV.UK Frontend:

- CSS
- JavaScript
- fonts
- images
- assets

Do not recreate these.

Use official fixture data and expected HTML from the same version.

## Understanding components

Before implementing a component:

1. inspect all fixtures for the component
2. inspect expected HTML
3. inspect upstream Nunjucks where useful
4. understand all options and interactions
5. design an idiomatic Django API
6. implement the component
7. execute the complete applicable fixture suite

Do not design the API around only the first or simplest fixture.

## Django architecture

Implement GOV.UK components as a reusable Django application.

Prefer native Django composition mechanisms.

Use custom template tags and inclusion tags where appropriate.

Suggested structure:

```text
govuk_components/
  components/
    button.py
    input.py
    radios.py
  templatetags/
    govuk_components.py
  templates/
    govuk/
      components/
        button.html
        input.html
        radios.html
  rendering/
  fixtures/

tests/
  components/
  fixtures/
  rendering/
```

Use dataclasses, TypedDicts, enums, or normal typed classes where they improve clarity.

Use type hints throughout application code.

Do not create elaborate class hierarchies merely to imitate another ecosystem.

## Component API

Component APIs should closely represent GOV.UK Frontend options while feeling natural to Django developers.

For example:

```django
{% load govuk_components %}

{% govuk_button button %}
```

with a typed Python representation of the options.

Keep HTTP views separate from component rendering.

Components must be testable without making an HTTP request.

## Templates

Use Django Templates.

Do not use Nunjucks.

Do not invoke Node.js to generate component HTML.

Keep template logic straightforward.

Move complicated transformation logic into Python.

Do not introduce extra markup for template convenience.

## Escaping and trusted HTML

Use Django automatic escaping.

GOV.UK `text` properties must be escaped.

GOV.UK `html` properties require explicit trusted HTML handling.

Use `mark_safe` only inside tightly controlled APIs where content has explicitly been classified as trusted.

Never apply `mark_safe` to arbitrary user input.

Do not disable autoescaping globally.

Do not weaken escaping to satisfy fixtures without understanding the expected behaviour.

## Attributes

Support arbitrary GOV.UK component attributes where defined.

Ensure values are escaped appropriately.

Preserve:

- IDs
- names
- values
- classes
- ARIA
- data attributes
- roles
- boolean attributes
- form semantics

## Fixture testing

Use PyYAML to load fixtures.

Tests should be parameterised using pytest.

For each fixture:

1. load upstream fixture data
2. map options into the local representation
3. render using Django Templates
4. obtain expected GOV.UK HTML
5. parse actual HTML
6. parse expected HTML
7. compare meaningful DOM structure
8. provide a useful failure diff

A new fixture should normally run automatically without another manually written test.

## HTML parsing and comparison

Use BeautifulSoup with html5lib or another standards-aware HTML parser.

Do not rely solely on raw string equality.

Normalise only genuinely irrelevant serialisation differences.

Never normalise away differences involving:

- elements
- ordering
- text
- classes
- IDs
- attributes
- values
- ARIA
- data attributes
- form semantics
- accessibility relationships

Document normalisation.

Test the comparator.

Assume the component is incorrect before changing comparator behaviour.

## Testing

Test:

- every supported fixture
- text escaping
- trusted HTML
- classes
- arbitrary attributes
- IDs
- ARIA
- errors
- hints
- nested content
- checked/selected states
- complex form components

Use pytest and pytest-django.

## Forbidden shortcuts

Never:

- modify upstream fixtures
- modify expected HTML
- special-case fixture names
- hard-code expected output
- copy fixture HTML directly into templates
- weaken DOM comparison to hide differences
- invoke Nunjucks at runtime
- invoke Node.js to render production HTML
- call another implementation at runtime
- introduce React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, or another SPA framework for core rendering
- replace GOV.UK Frontend CSS or JavaScript unnecessarily

Unsupported fixtures should remain visible as failures or explicitly documented exclusions.

## Progressive enhancement

Server-render the complete core experience.

Use official GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript for progressive enhancement.

Do not make JavaScript responsible for constructing essential initial component markup.

## Dependencies

Use normal Python dependency management for the project.

Pin dependencies reproducibly.

Keep dependencies minimal.

Use established libraries.

Run dependency/security tooling where appropriate.

## Quality

Use:

```text
ruff
mypy
pytest
pytest-django
```

Prefer:

- small functions
- explicit types
- dataclasses where useful
- standard Django conventions
- simple templates
- composition

Avoid:

- excessive metaprogramming
- HTML string concatenation
- indiscriminate `mark_safe`
- unnecessary JavaScript
- TypeScript-style architecture copied into Python

## CI

CI must:

1. install reproducible dependencies
2. run Ruff
3. run mypy
4. run pytest
5. run the complete fixture parity suite

Parity failures fail CI.

## GOV.UK Frontend upgrades

When upgrading GOV.UK Frontend:

1. update the pinned version
2. update the fixture set
3. run all existing tests
4. inspect parity failures
5. modify Django rendering where upstream behaviour changed
6. rerun everything

Do not modify fixtures to accommodate the old implementation.

## Definition of done

A component is complete when:

- Django server-renders it
- no Nunjucks runtime rendering exists
- no Node.js runtime rendering exists
- official GOV.UK assets are used
- every supported fixture passes
- escaping is tested
- trusted HTML is tested
- accessibility markup matches
- no fixture-specific hacks exist
- CI passes

Fixture parity defines compatibility.
